Plan: PostgreSQL Data Architecture Redesign
TL;DR: Redesign the backend to store all extracted data in PostgreSQL instead of memory, enabling persistent data (survives refresh), scalability (10M+ rows), multi-user access with permissions, and efficient pagination.
Implementation Steps
Phase 1: Database Schema & Migrations (Week 1)
Add 6 new models to backend/models.py:
Worksheet — metadata (name, owner, sharing settings)
WorksheetColumn — column schema + statistics (null counts, min/max)
WorksheetData — actual data rows stored as JSON
WorksheetPermission — access control (who can view/edit)
DataImportQueue — progress tracking for large uploads
WorksheetTransformation — staging area for transformations
Create Alembic migration and run alembic upgrade head
Verifiable: psql shows 6 new tables with proper indexes
Phase 2: Data Import Service (Week 1-2)
Create backend/services/worksheet_service.py:
import_data_from_file(file_path, worksheet_id) — reads CSV in 10K-row chunks
calculate_column_statistics(worksheet_id) — null/unique/min/max
update_import_progress(worksheet_id, rows_processed)
Modify backend/routers/files.py:
File upload now calls worksheet_service → stores in DB instead of memory
Verifiable: Upload CSV → check worksheet_data table has rows
Phase 3: Query Service with Pagination (Week 2)
Create backend/services/query_service.py:
get_worksheet_data(worksheet_id, user_id, offset=0, limit=100) — paginated rows
filter_data(worksheet_id, user_id, filters) — WHERE clauses from UI
sort_data(worksheet_id, user_id, sort_by, order) — ORDER BY
aggregate_data(worksheet_id, user_id, group_by, agg_type) — SUM, COUNT, AVG
Implement permission checks:
Query WorksheetPermission before returning data
Return 403 if user not authorized
Support filter operators: =, !=, >, <, >=, <=, CONTAINS, IN
Phase 4: API Endpoints (Week 2)
Create backend/routers/worksheets.py:
GET    /worksheets              → list user's worksheets
GET    /worksheets/{id}/data    → paginated data (with filters, sorting)
POST   /worksheets/{id}/share   → add user permission
DELETE /worksheets/{id}/permissions/{perm_id} → revoke access
GET    /worksheets/{id}/export  → download CSV
Update backend/routers/reports.py:
refreshElementData() queries /worksheets/{id}/data instead of cache
Phase 5: Frontend Integration (Week 2-3)
Update frontend/src/pages/PaginatedReportBuilder.jsx:
Query new /worksheets/{id}/data endpoint with pagination params
Support infinite scroll / pagination controls for 10K+ rows
Add share/permission UI to select users and permission levels
Phase 6: Connector Integration (Week 3)
Update all connectors (backend/connectors/):
Instead of returning DataFrame → call worksheet_service.import_data_from_dataframe(df)
Store results in Worksheet instead of memory
Track progress for large datasets (100K+ rows)
Phase 7: Migration & Cleanup (Week 3-4)
Migrate existing UploadedFile data to new Worksheet schema
Keep old UploadedFile as archive (linked via foreign key)
Update existing reports to reference new Worksheet IDs
Performance tuning: add indexes, ANALYZE tables, test with 1M+ rows
Performance Expectations
Dataset Size	Query Time (with pagination)
1K rows	< 10ms
10K rows	10-50ms
100K rows	50-200ms
1M rows	200-500ms
10M rows	500ms-2s
 
 
Key Architecture Decisions
Why JSON for data storage? Flexible (any column type), efficient JSONB indexing, no schema changes for new columns
Why separate tables? Worksheet metadata separate from actual rows → scalability + archival capability
Why WorksheetPermission? User/org-wide access control + audit trail of who granted access
Why DataImportQueue? Progress tracking for large uploads + failure recovery